=> Git Branching, Pull Request, Approval and Merge Workflow

    -> Purpose
        -> This workflow is used when multiple developers are working on the same repository.
        -> Each developer works on a separate branch instead of directly modifying main.
        -> Changes are pushed to GitHub and merged into main only through a Pull Request.
        -> A reviewer/owner approves the Pull Request before the developer merges it.


=> Prerequisites

    -> Git must be installed.

        git --version
        -> Checks whether Git is installed.


    -> GitHub CLI should be installed if Pull Requests will be created from CMD.

        gh --version
        -> Checks whether GitHub CLI is installed.


    -> If GitHub CLI is not installed on Windows:

        winget install --id GitHub.cli
        -> Installs GitHub CLI using Windows Package Manager.


    -> Developer must be authenticated with GitHub CLI.

        gh auth login
        -> Starts GitHub CLI login process.


        gh auth status
        -> Verifies which GitHub account is currently authenticated.


    -> Developer must have permission to push to the repository.

        Example:

        Repository Owner:
        Vinay8386

        Collaborator:
        Akansha8386

    -> Repository owner must add the developer as a collaborator.


    -> Git commit identity should be configured.

        git config user.name "Akansha"
        -> Sets the Git commit author name for the current repository.


        git config user.email "akansha-email@example.com"
        -> Sets the Git commit author email for the current repository.


        git config user.name
        -> Verifies configured Git username.


        git config user.email
        -> Verifies configured Git email.


    -> For a shared laptop, avoid --global if different users work on different repositories.


=> Step 1 – Verify Repository

    -> Move to the repository folder first.

        cd D:\Vinay_git_repo\core_Java
        -> Opens the required Git repository folder.


        git status
        -> Verifies that the folder is a Git repository and shows current branch status.


=> Step 2 – Check Existing Branches

        git branch
        -> Displays all local branches and marks the current branch with *.


        git branch -r
        -> Displays all remote branches available on GitHub.


=> Step 3 – Switch to Main Branch

        git checkout main
        -> Switches the working directory to the main branch.


    -> If git branch already shows:

        * main

    -> Then git checkout main is not required again.


=> Step 4 – Update Main Before Starting New Work

        git pull origin main
        -> Downloads and integrates the latest main branch changes from GitHub.


    -> Always perform this before creating a new working branch.

    -> This ensures the new branch starts from the latest version of main.


=> Step 5 – Create a New Working Branch

        git checkout -b git_file_updates
        -> Creates a new branch named git_file_updates and switches to it.


        git branch
        -> Verifies that the new branch is currently active.


    -> Example output:

        * git_file_updates
          main


=> Step 6 – Make Required Code Changes

    -> Add, modify or delete the required files.

    -> After making changes:

        git status
        -> Displays modified, deleted and untracked files.


=> Step 7 – Stage the Changes

        git add .
        -> Stages all current changes for the next commit.


    -> To stage only one file:

        git add "Git Branching & Merging Workflow"
        -> Stages only the specified file.


        git status
        -> Verifies that the required files are staged.


=> Step 8 – Commit the Changes

        git commit -m "Updated Git file"
        -> Creates a local commit containing the staged changes.


        git log --oneline
        -> Displays commit history in short format for verification.


=> Step 9 – Push the New Branch to GitHub

        git push -u origin git_file_updates
        -> Pushes the local branch to GitHub and sets its upstream branch.


    -> -u means:

        Local:
        git_file_updates

        Linked with:

        Remote:
        origin/git_file_updates


    -> After the first push, future updates can normally use:

        git push
        -> Pushes new commits to the already configured remote branch.


    -> Important:

        Before the first push:
        -> Branch exists only locally.

        After the first push:
        -> Branch becomes visible on GitHub.


=> Step 10 – Create Pull Request

        gh pr create --base main --head git_file_updates --title "Updated Git Branching Workflow" --body "Updated Git branching, pull request, approval and merge workflow notes."
        -> Creates a Pull Request from git_file_updates into main.


    -> Meaning:

        --base main
        -> Specifies the destination branch.


        --head git_file_updates
        -> Specifies the source branch.


        --title
        -> Specifies the Pull Request title.


        --body
        -> Specifies the Pull Request description.


=> Step 11 – Verify Pull Request

        gh pr view
        -> Displays the Pull Request associated with the current branch.


    -> Or:

        gh pr view <PR-NUMBER>
        -> Displays a specific Pull Request.


    -> Example:

        gh pr view 1
        -> Displays Pull Request number 1.


=> Step 12 – Repository Owner Reviews the Pull Request

    -> Repository owner opens:

        GitHub Repository
            ->
        Pull Requests
            ->
        Required Pull Request
            ->
        Files changed
            ->
        Review changes
            ->
        Approve
            ->
        Submit review


    -> Example result:

        Reviewers:
        Vinay8386 (Approved)


=> Step 13 – Verify Approval from Developer Side

        gh pr view <PR-NUMBER>
        -> Checks whether the Pull Request has received the required approval.


    -> Example:

        gh pr view 1


    -> Expected status:

        Reviewers: Vinay8386 (Approved)


=> Step 14 – Merge Pull Request from Developer Side

        gh pr merge <PR-NUMBER>
        -> Starts the merge process for the approved Pull Request.


    -> Example:

        gh pr merge 1
        -> Merges Pull Request number 1 after all repository rules are satisfied.


    -> If GitHub asks for merge method:

        Merge commit
        -> Creates a merge commit and preserves the branch commit history.


        Squash and merge
        -> Combines all branch commits into a single commit.


        Rebase and merge
        -> Places branch commits directly on top of main without a merge commit.


    -> For the current workflow:

        Merge commit
        -> Recommended for a clear collaborative branch history.


=> Step 15 – Switch Back to Main After Merge

        git checkout main
        -> Switches the local working directory back to main.


=> Step 16 – Download the Newly Merged Code

        git pull origin main
        -> Updates local main with the changes that were merged through the Pull Request.


        git status
        -> Verifies that local main is synchronized with origin/main.


    -> Expected:

        On branch main
        Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.
        nothing to commit, working tree clean


=> Step 17 – Delete the Completed Local Branch

        git branch -d git_file_updates
        -> Safely deletes the completed local working branch.


    -> Use -d when the branch has already been merged.


    -> Avoid -D unless forced deletion is intentionally required.


=> Step 18 – Delete the Completed Remote Branch

        git push origin --delete git_file_updates
        -> Deletes the completed branch from GitHub.


    -> If GitHub automatically deleted the branch after merge, this command is not required.


=> Step 19 – Verify Branch Deletion

        git branch
        -> Verifies that the local working branch has been deleted.


        git branch -r
        -> Verifies that the remote working branch has been deleted.


    -> Expected final local branch:

        * main


=> Step 20 – Start the Next Task

        git checkout main
        -> Ensures the developer is working from main.


        git pull origin main
        -> Downloads the latest approved and merged code.


        git checkout -b new-task-branch
        -> Creates a fresh branch for the next task.


    -> A completed branch should normally not be reused for a new task.


=> Complete Daily Development Flow

        git checkout main
        -> Switch to main.


        git pull origin main
        -> Update main with the latest remote changes.


        git checkout -b new-task-branch
        -> Create a fresh branch for the new task.


        git status
        -> Check modified and untracked files.


        git add .
        -> Stage the required changes.


        git commit -m "Updated required functionality"
        -> Commit the staged changes.


        git push -u origin new-task-branch
        -> Push the new branch to GitHub.


        gh pr create --base main --head new-task-branch --title "Updated required functionality" --body "Updated required functionality."
        -> Create a Pull Request into main.


        gh pr view
        -> Check Pull Request and approval status.


        gh pr merge
        -> Merge the Pull Request after required approval.


        git checkout main
        -> Return to main after merge.


        git pull origin main
        -> Download the newly merged code.


        git branch -d new-task-branch
        -> Delete the completed local branch.


        git push origin --delete new-task-branch
        -> Delete the completed remote branch if required.


=> GitHub Main Branch Protection

    -> Recommended Ruleset

        Require a pull request before merging
        -> Prevents direct changes from being merged into main without a Pull Request.


        Required approvals: 1
        -> Requires at least one eligible reviewer approval before merge.


        Dismiss stale pull request approvals when new commits are pushed
        -> Removes previous approval if new code is pushed after approval.


    -> Example:

        Akansha creates PR
            ->
        Vinay approves
            ->
        Akansha pushes another commit
            ->
        Previous approval is dismissed
            ->
        Vinay must review and approve again.


    -> For the current workflow, keep this disabled:

        Require approval of the most recent reviewable push

    -> Reason:

        If Vinay himself pushes the latest commit in his own branch,
        another reviewer could be required to approve that latest push.


